course: religion-ethics
lesson: 9
title: King David and King Solomon
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: David became Israel's greatest king: he made Jerusalem his capital and danced with joy before God, but he also did a terrible wrong when he took Bathsheba, another man's wife, and arranged for her husband Uriah to be killed in battle.
explain: The prophet Nathan told David a story about a rich man who stole a poor man's only lamb; David was furious, and Nathan said, «You are the man»; David admitted his sin and prayed Psalm 51, «Create in me a clean heart».
explain: David's son Solomon, offered anything by God, asked for wisdom to rule well instead of riches, and he showed it when two women both claimed one baby: the real mother gave the baby up rather than let it be harmed.
explain: Solomon built the first great Temple in Jerusalem, but in later life he turned to other gods and taxed his people heavily, and after him the kingdom split in two.
explain: For a child today this means: heroes can do wrong, an honest sorry is where change begins, a friend who tells you the truth is a gift, and wisdom has to be lived, not only known.
fact: David became Israel's greatest king and made Jerusalem his capital.
fact: David danced with joy before God when the Ark of the Covenant was brought to Jerusalem.
fact: David took Bathsheba, another man's wife, and arranged for her husband Uriah to be killed in battle.
fact: The prophet Nathan told David a story of a rich man stealing a poor man's lamb, then said «You are the man».
fact: David admitted his sin, and Psalm 51 is the prayer of sorrow linked with him: «Create in me a clean heart, O God».
fact: When God offered Solomon anything, he asked for wisdom to rule well, not riches or a long life, and God gave him riches as well.
fact: Two women claimed one baby; Solomon ordered it cut in half, and the true mother gave it up to save its life.
fact: Solomon built the first great Temple in Jerusalem as a house for worshipping God, yet he prayed that even the heavens cannot contain God, much less a house.
fact: In later life Solomon turned to other gods and taxed his people heavily.
fact: After Solomon the kingdom split into Israel in the north and Judah in the south.
fact: Christians believe that God forgives people who truly repent, even after great wrongs.
fact: Historians debate how large and rich the kingdoms of David and Solomon really were; this is disputed.
exercise: Which city did David make his capital? | Jerusalem | Bethlehem | Babylon | Nineveh
exercise: What did David do when the Ark was brought to Jerusalem? | He danced with joy before God | He hid it away in a dark mountain cave | He sold it to the king of Egypt for gold | He forbade anyone in the city to see it
exercise: What terrible wrong did David do with Bathsheba and Uriah? | He took another man's wife and arranged for her husband to die | He stole Uriah's sheep and goats | He burned down Uriah's house | He sent Bathsheba away to Egypt
exercise: What does David's wrong teach about heroes? | Even heroes can do terrible wrongs, and power can tempt people | Heroes never do anything wrong | Kings are allowed to break the law, because they are the ones who make it | Only bad people ever sin
exercise: What was Nathan's story about? | A rich man who stole a poor man's only lamb | A shepherd who lost one of his hundred sheep and went to look for it | A king who built a tower | A son who ran away from home
exercise: What did Nathan say when David grew angry at the rich man? | You are the man | Forgive the rich man | The lamb was only a story | Let the poor man pay
exercise: Why did Nathan tell a story instead of accusing David directly? | So David would see his own wrong for himself | Because Nathan was far too afraid to speak plainly | Because David loved hearing jokes and funny tales | Because telling stories to kings was the law then
exercise: What did David do after Nathan spoke? | He admitted his sin and asked God for a clean heart | He threw Nathan in prison | He blamed Bathsheba | He said nothing had happened
exercise: Which psalm is the prayer of sorrow linked with David? | Psalm 51 | Psalm 23 | Psalm 100 | Psalm 1
exercise: What did Solomon ask God for? | Wisdom to rule his people well | Great riches and piles of gold and silver | A very long and healthy life for himself | Victory over all of his enemies
exercise: How did Solomon find the true mother? | He saw which woman would give up the baby to save its life | He asked the neighbours to vote | He looked closely at the baby to see whose face it looked like | He gave the baby to the older woman
exercise: What did Solomon build in Jerusalem? | The first great Temple | The city's first wall | A tower to reach the sky | A palace for Pharaoh
exercise: Why can God not be «contained» in the Temple, as Solomon himself prayed? | God is greater than any building | The Temple was much too small for him | God lived only out in the desert, far away | The Temple was never actually finished
exercise: How did Solomon fail in later life? | He turned to other gods and taxed his people heavily | He lost all of his wisdom in a single day and became foolish | He refused to build the Temple | He gave away his kingdom to Egypt
exercise: What happened to the kingdom after Solomon? | It split into Israel in the north and Judah in the south | It became part of Egypt | It grew twice as large | It was ruled by Nathan
exercise: What do Christians believe about someone who truly repents? | God forgives them, even after great wrongs | Some wrongs can never be forgiven by God | Only kings and priests can be forgiven, not ordinary people | Saying sorry changes nothing
test: My friend says Solomon asked God to make him the richest king ever. Is that right? | No, he asked for wisdom, and God added riches too | Yes, he asked only for gold | No, he asked God for a much longer life than other kings | Yes, and then he asked for an army
test: «so the king said cut the baby lol that's psycho». What was Solomon really doing? | Testing which woman truly loved the baby | Punishing both women for lying | Following an old law about babies that Moses had written | Making a cruel joke at a feast
test: A Hindu classmate asks: «if David did such bad things, why do Christians call him great?» | Because he admitted his wrong, repented and kept trusting God | Because Christians think he never did anything wrong | Because he was the richest man alive | Because he defeated Goliath, so nothing else counts
test: you broke mum's vase and your sister says «just say the cat did it». Which story warns against this? | David, who tried to hide his wrong until Nathan showed it to him | Solomon, who prayed for wisdom and built the great Temple in Jerusalem | Ruth and Naomi | Jonah and the plant
test: Your best mate tells you straight «you were really mean to Ella today». Who is your mate like? | Nathan, who told David the truth | Goliath, who mocked the whole army | Pharaoh, who would not listen to Moses | Saul, who was jealous of young David
test: «solomon was the wisest guy ever so everything he did was right». What is wrong with this? | Wisdom must be lived; Solomon later turned to other gods and was harsh | Nothing, Solomon never did wrong | Solomon was not wise at all | Solomon was only wise as a child
test: big sis: «the kingdom split cos of a war with Egypt». Better answer? | After Solomon, harsh rule and pride split it into Israel and Judah | Egypt took half of it | Goliath's people divided it between their own kings after a great battle | Nathan split it between his sons
test: «i said sorry 5 times, so it's done, right?» What does Psalm 51 add? | A real sorry asks for a changed, clean heart, not just words | Saying it five times is enough | Sorry only counts in church | You should never say sorry
test: A judge asks: «Why did Nathan use a story instead of shouting at the king?» | A story let David judge the wrong before seeing it was his own | Nathan liked to entertain the king | Shouting was forbidden in the palace | David could not understand plain words, only stories and songs and riddles
test: A judge follows up: «Do historians agree how big Solomon's kingdom was?» | No, historians debate its size and wealth; it is disputed | Yes, it covered all of Africa | Yes, it was bigger than Rome | No, because Solomon never had a kingdom in any account
test: A classmate with no faith says: «why build a fancy church, God's everywhere anyway?» A fair answer? | A beautiful building honours God, though Christians agree God cannot be contained in it | Because God lives only inside churches | Because the Bible says all churches must be gold | Because people cannot pray anywhere else
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the prophet Nathan recounted to King David the narrative of the poor man's lamb. | To cause the king to recognise and condemn his own wrongdoing | To request compensation for a stolen animal on behalf of a poor man | To entertain the royal court at a festival | To announce the construction of the Temple
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the petition made by Solomon upon being offered a gift by God? | He requested wisdom for the governance of his people | He requested the death of his enemies | He requested riches exceeding those of all kings before and after him | He requested a reign of unlimited duration
exam: It is given that two women each asserted maternity of a single infant. Determine the means by which Solomon ascertained the true mother. | He observed which claimant would relinquish the child to preserve its life | He consulted the records of the Temple | He awarded the child to the elder claimant | He required each woman to swear a solemn oath before the priests of the Temple
exam: State the consequence for the kingdom which ensued subsequent to the reign of Solomon. | Its division into Israel in the north and Judah in the south | Its absorption, in its entirety, into the empire of Egypt under Pharaoh | Its expansion to the River Euphrates | Its transfer to the house of Saul
